motorcycle riders are 28 times more  likely to die in a crash than someone in  a car that's according to the national  highway safety traffic Administration  just this week a rider died in North Las  Vegas in a hit and run these crashes  leading to some call to call for change  rather Joshua piguero spoke to a man who  wants local lawmakers to intervene  Josh talbus is a veteran and a long time  motorcycle rider who decided last year  he's no longer riding on Valley streets  you can be as defensive as you want  you're always looking for a driver to  pull out in front of you but you only  have a split second and sometimes you  just don't have that reaction time and  if somebody's going to pull out in front  of you I mean there's sometimes there's  no avoidance Tapas launched a change.org  petition to call attention to this issue  he has more than 1500 signatures and it  continues to grow Tapas says he wants  harsher penalties for those who crash  into motorcyclists just seeing so many  people especially here in Vegas getting  killed I mean especially car versus  motorcycle it seems like every at least  once a week this week police say a moped  with two passengers was rear-ended in  North Las Vegas at the intersection of  Belmont Street and Dunn Avenue  both weren't wearing helmets and a woman  writer was pronounced dead at the scene  investigators aren't asking for help in  finding the pickup truck that hit them  these motorcycle riders are learning  behaviors that will reduce their chances  of being involved in a crash rather than  eliminated  the College of Southern Nevada has a  beginner's motorcycle course Lori  Sanders is one of the instructors  whether it's a car whether it's a  motorcycle many of the crashes that  happen are a result of somebody's poor  decision poor choice the course is five  hours of online learning and two days of  meeting and writing Sanders says  motorcyclists can protect themselves by  wearing a helmet and cars by just  slowing down how 